 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Ray Casting SART/SIRT
-An alternative implementation of SART/SIRT proximal operators based on ray casting [S1]. In this variant, threads compute line integrals and correction terms for forward projection by casting rays from the detector through the volume, and update voxels based on correction images for back-projection [S1]. Forward projection involves casting a ray from each pixel, sampling the volume at equidistant points, and retrieving volume values via tri-linear interpolation [S1]. The correction term is computed as $(I(p_i) - \text{sample\_rate} \times \sum_{s=1}^k v_{sk}) / \text{ray\_length}$, where $I(p_i)$ is the measured line integral, $S$ is the number of samples, $\text{sample\_rate}$ is the distance between samples, $v_{sk}$ is the volume estimate at sample $s$, and $\text{ray\_length}$ is the ray-volume intersection length [S1]. Back-projection involves casting a ray from each voxel to the detector, retrieving a correction value via bi-linear interpolation, and updating the voxel [S1]. In this way, the forward projection and backward projection are not exact [S1].
+An alternative implementation of SART/SIRT proximal operators based on ray casting [S1]. In this variant, threads compute line integrals and correction terms for forward projection by casting rays from the detector through the volume, and update voxels based on correction images for back-projection [S1]. Forward projection involves casting a ray from each pixel, sampling the volume at equidistant points, and retrieving volume values via tri-linear interpolation [S1]. The correction term is computed as $(I(p_i) - \text{sample\_rate} \times \sum_{s=1}^S v_{sk}) / \text{ray\_length}$, where $I(p_i)$ is the measured line integral, $S$ is the number of samples, $\text{sample\_rate}$ is the distance between samples, $v_{sk}$ is the volume estimate at sample $s$, and $\text{ray\_length}$ is the ray-volume intersection length [S1]. Back-projection involves casting a ray from each voxel to the detector, retrieving a correction value via bi-linear interpolation, and updating the voxel [S1]. In this way, the forward projection and backward projection are not exact [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The complexity is proportional to the number of voxels times the kernel support size for forward projection, and the number of detector pixels for correction image computation. Back-projection is proportional to the number of voxels times the kernel support size. Overall, it is $O(N_v \cdot K + N_p)$, where $N_v$ is the number of voxels, $K$ is the kernel support size, and $N_p$ is the number of detector pixels [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the volume, two 2D images (ray-sum and weights), and the correction image. The LUT size depends on the resolution of the pre-computed weights [S1].
